<p>You cut into a potato that looked perfectly normal and find a grey, brown or almost black patch in the flesh. The obvious question is: <strong>is the potato rotten, or can it still be used?</strong> The answer depends on the appearance, texture and extent of the dark area and on whether there are any other signs of spoilage.</p>
<p>One common cause is <strong>internal blackspot bruising</strong>. Potatoes can be damaged mechanically during harvesting, packing, transport or storage even when the skin looks intact. Injured cells undergo oxidation reactions and dark pigments form over the following hours or days, so the damage may only become visible when the tuber is peeled or cut.</p>
<h2>A black bruise is not the same as rot</h2>
<p>A typical internal bruise is usually <strong>dry, firm and localised</strong>. It is often close to the skin and does not make the flesh wet, slimy or soft. The potato should not smell abnormal.</p>
<p>Rot is different. Spoiled tissue is more likely to be soft, watery, sunken, leaking, foul-smelling or poorly defined as it spreads through the tuber. Colour alone is not enough: <strong>texture, smell and extent matter as much as darkness</strong>.</p>
<h2>Why bruised potato flesh turns black</h2>
<p>Potato cells contain phenolic compounds and enzymes that are normally compartmentalised. An impact damages cell structures, allowing those components to interact and oxidise. Dark pigments then form in the injured area.</p>
<p>The colour can take a day or more to develop. A tuber may therefore be bruised during harvest or transport and still arrive in the kitchen without any obvious external mark.</p>
<h2>Can you eat a potato with a small black spot?</h2>
<p>If the potato is firm, smells normal and the dark area is <strong>small, dry and clearly limited</strong>, it is reasonable to cut away the affected tissue generously and use the rest promptly. The bruised part is normally removed for quality because its appearance and texture are poor.</p>
<p>There is no need to rescue every potato at all costs. If several large dark areas appear throughout the flesh or the condition of the tuber is uncertain, discarding one potato is more sensible than trying to interpret every mark.</p>
<h2>Dark patches just beneath the skin</h2>
<p>Mechanical blackspot frequently appears a few millimetres below the skin as a grey or black area with diffuse edges. Sometimes the outside is slightly flattened by pressure; sometimes there is no visible clue.</p>
<p>Peeling a little deeper shows whether the damage is limited. If normal, firm flesh appears beneath the patch, the situation is very different from decay that continues into soft tissue.</p>
<h2>Dry brown spots deeper inside</h2>
<p>Not every internal dark defect is caused by impact. Potatoes can also develop <strong>dry, corky brown or black sectors</strong> because of physiological problems during growth, including irregular water supply and related nutrient disorders. These areas are usually dry rather than wet.</p>
<p>For a household cook, the exact agronomic name matters less than the practical distinction between firm, dry tissue and decomposing tissue. If a defect occupies a large part of the potato, quality is poor enough that discarding it is normally the best choice.</p>
<h2>What about a dark hollow in the centre?</h2>
<p>Some tubers develop internal cavities because of uneven growth. A dry cavity with firm surrounding flesh and no abnormal smell can be a physiological quality defect rather than microbial rot.</p>
<p>A wet, slimy, foul-smelling cavity is another matter. As always, judge the condition of the surrounding flesh instead of relying only on shape or colour.</p>
<h2>When should the potato be discarded?</h2>
<ul>
<li>If the flesh around the dark area is <strong>soft, wet, slimy or collapsing</strong>.</li>
<li>If there is a clearly unpleasant or fermented smell.</li>
<li>If darkening is extensive through much of the tuber.</li>
<li>If visible mould, leaking fluid or active decay is present.</li>
<li>If the entire potato is badly shrivelled or generally deteriorated.</li>
</ul>
<p>Reducing food waste does not mean assuming that every damaged food can be made safe by trimming.</p>
<h2>Do not confuse black spots with green potatoes</h2>
<p>Internal black bruising and greening are different phenomena. Green colour develops after light exposure and can be associated with increased natural glycoalkaloids. Our broader guide to <a href="/en/potatoes-which-type-to-choose-for-frying-boiling-roasting-tortilla-or-mash/">choosing and storing potatoes</a> covers greening, sprouting and general storage. This article focuses specifically on dark internal spots.</p>
<h2>Does cooking solve the problem?</h2>
<p>Cooking changes texture and colour, but it does not make a rotten potato acceptable. A small dry bruise is removed before cooking for quality. If the potato shows signs of decomposition, the decision to discard it should be made <strong>before</strong> boiling, roasting or frying.</p>
<p>Bruised areas can also look darker after cooking. The heat has not necessarily created a new problem; it can simply make existing tissue damage more visible.</p>
<h2>How to reduce bruising at home</h2>
<p>Potatoes look robust but they can be injured by hard drops, crushing and heavy pressure. Handle bags carefully and avoid storing heavy objects on top of the tubers.</p>
<p>Keep potatoes in a cool, dark, dry, ventilated place and inspect them periodically. Use mildly damaged but otherwise sound potatoes first. Our guide to <a href="/en/how-to-store-vegetables-what-goes-in-the-fridge-and-what-does-not/">storing vegetables properly</a> explains the broader principles.</p>
<h2>A simple three-point check</h2>
<p>When you discover a dark area, assess three things. First, <strong>extent</strong>: a small localised patch is different from widespread blackening. Second, texture: the flesh should remain firm. Third, smell: a mechanical bruise should not produce a rotten odour.</p>
<p>If all three checks are reassuring and the spot is small, trimming is reasonable. If several warning signs appear together, discard the tuber.</p>
<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
<h3>Is a black spot inside a potato toxic?</h3>
<p>A small internal bruise is not, by itself, evidence of toxicity. It is normally removed for quality. The assessment changes if the potato also shows rot, extensive greening or other deterioration.</p>
<h3>Why was there no mark on the outside?</h3>
<p>The skin can remain intact while the impact damages cells beneath it. Dark pigmentation develops later and is only revealed when the potato is cut.</p>
<h3>Can I cut the black part off and fry the rest?</h3>
<p>Yes, when the dark area is small and dry and the remaining potato is firm and normal. Remove the affected flesh with a reasonable margin and use the rest promptly.</p>
<h3>Is internal blackening the same as a green potato?</h3>
<p>No. Greening is associated with light exposure, while blackspot bruising is usually linked to mechanical impact or pressure.</p>
